    "alert_id": "TDF_FANCONI_SYNDROME_RISK",
    "title": "TDF toxicity signs with low phosphate and bone pain",
    "message": "Guidelines list TDF as causing Fanconi syndrome, decreased bone mineral density, and musculoskeletal symptoms including lower back pain, bone aches, and muscle cramps. Management: do LFTs and RFTs; if deranged (GFR <60), substitute with ABC. Patient on TDF for 6 years presents with lower back pain and heavy thighs worsening over 4 months, serum phosphate 0.78 mmol/L (low), trace proteinuria, and creatinine 112 umol/L. Evidence for drug attribution: TDF is directly linked to proximal tubular dysfunction (Fanconi syndrome) causing hypophosphataemia, proteinuria, and bone/muscle pain. Evidence against: back pain may be occupational from truck driving; proteinuria attributed to uncontrolled hypertension; creatinine is within normal limits. However, the combination of low phosphate, proteinuria, and bone pain on long-term TDF warrants evaluation for tubular toxicity.",
    "issue_type": "toxicity",
    "severity": "high",
    "recommended_action": "Check eGFR and LFTs. If GFR <60 or LFTs deranged, substitute TDF with ABC. If ABC contraindicated, substitute with AZT. Consider renal phosphate reabsorption evaluation.",
